+/*
+ * ng_h4(4) -- Bluetooth H4 UART transport.
+ *
+ * This node attaches to an already-open tty via ttyhook_register(9), the
+ * same MPSAFE tty API ng_tty(4) uses.  It has a single hook, normally
+ * connected to ng_hci(4)'s "drv" hook.
+ *
+ * Wire format (Bluetooth Core spec, "UART Transport Layer" / H4): every
+ * frame starts with a single packet-indicator byte, one of
+ *
+ *	NG_HCI_CMD_PKT		0x01	command, host -> controller only
+ *	NG_HCI_ACL_DATA_PKT	0x02	ACL data, either direction
+ *	NG_HCI_SCO_DATA_PKT	0x03	SCO data, either direction
+ *	NG_HCI_EVENT_PKT	0x04	event, controller -> host only
+ *
+ * followed by a type-specific fixed header that ends in a length field,
+ * followed by that many bytes of payload.  ng_hci.h's ng_hci_cmd_pkt_t /
+ * ng_hci_acldata_pkt_t / ng_hci_scodata_pkt_t / ng_hci_event_pkt_t already
+ * describe these headers *including* the indicator byte, and their "type"
+ * values already match the wire values above -- ng_ubt.c prepends these
+ * same indicator bytes onto mbufs before handing them to ng_hci.  So an
+ * mbuf handed to/from ng_hci here is in exactly the form it
+ * needs to be in on the wire; no repacking, only stream reassembly.
+ *
+ * We never originate or expect NG_HCI_CMD_PKT on the receive (tty -> HCI)
+ * side -- only the controller-to-host types (ACL/SCO/EVENT) are legal
+ * there.  A byte that isn't a legal indicator where the reassembler
+ * expects one is treated as a framing error: the byte is dropped and we
+ * stay waiting for the next indicator.  There is no way to recover framing
+ * lost *inside* a header or payload other than starting over at the next
+ * byte that happens to look like an indicator, which may itself be wrong.
+ *
+ * NOTE ON LOCKING: this node runs in two contexts.
+ *
+ * 1) tty context.  ngh4_rint()/ngh4_rint_bypass() (receive) and
+ *    ngh4_getc_inject()/ngh4_getc_poll() (transmit) are called by the tty
+ *    layer with the tty locked (tty_assert_locked()).  All of the RX
+ *    reassembly state in sc (rx_*) is touched *only* from this context,
+ *    so it needs no lock of its own -- the tty lock already serializes
+ *    it, exactly as ng_tty.c's per-mbuf RX state is tty-lock-serialized.
+ *
+ * 2) Netgraph context.  ngh4_rcvdata() (frames arriving from ng_hci to be
+ *    transmitted) runs here, and reaches into the tty to call
+ *    ttydevsw_outwakeup() -- i.e. it *enters* tty context, which is why
+ *    ngh4_connect() forces our own hook into queuing mode
+ *    (NG_HOOK_FORCE_QUEUE()), exactly as ng_tty.c's ngt_connect() does:
+ *    it keeps us from being called with some other node's WRITER lock
+ *    held while we go touch the tty.
+ *
+ * sc->outq (outgoing frames waiting for the tty) is the only field shared
+ * between contexts 1 and 2 (getc_inject/getc_poll dequeue it from tty
+ * context, rcvdata enqueues it from Netgraph context), so it alone gets
+ * a lock: its own embedded ifq_mtx (see NGH4LOCK()), exactly as
+ * ng_tty.c's outq+ifq_mtx.
+ *
+ * sc->tp and sc->hook are set once (SET_TTY message, newhook) and cleared
+ * once (shutdown, disconnect); ng_tty.c accepts the same small race on tp.
+ */
+
